## Bug B: De Morgan's Law Misimplementation (The Disjunction Error)
When throwing errors, developers have to negate the success condition. According to De Morgan's Laws, the negation of a conjunction is a disjunction ($\lor$):
$$\neg(A \land B) \equiv \neg A \lor \neg B$$ 
The system should trigger an error if:
$$(x < 105) \lor (x > 500000)$$ 

* The Bug: If the developer accidentally used an "AND" ($\land$) operator in the error validation line (e.g., if (amount < 105 && amount > 500000)), the statement becomes a contradiction. It is mathematically impossible for a number to be less than 105 and greater than 500,000 at the same time.
